# Nathejk Skan — Project Rules & Context

> Org-wide conventions live in `.agents/rules/rules.md` (Docker, Traefik,
> JetStream, git, task board). Read that too. This file describes **this repo**
> and where it deviates from the org standard.

---

## Overview

**Skan** is the QR scanning service for **Nathejk**, a Danish scouting night race
where patrols (*patruljer*) navigate from checkpoint to checkpoint while being
hunted by *banditter*.

Every patrol carries a paper map with a unique QR sticker on it. Patrols receive
new maps several times during the race, and **every map has its own QR code**.
Two things happen with those codes:

1. **Registration** — the first time a code is scanned, it is unknown to the
   system. The scanner is asked for the patrol's team number (the big number on
   the scouts' arms, *not* the small number next to the QR code), which binds
   that QR code to that patrol for the rest of its life.
2. **Scanning** — every later scan of that code records *who* scanned it, *when*,
   and *where* (browser geolocation, or a hand-typed map coordinate as fallback).

This repo is what a phone lands on when someone scans a sticker: the QR encodes a
URL into this service. An anonymous visitor is asked to log in with their phone
number; the number is matched against event personnel and clan seniors, which is
also how the app knows whether the scanner is a **bandit** or a **crew member** —
the two are meant to get different views.

Skan only *produces* scan facts. Aggregation, reporting and admin live in other
Nathejk services (notably `hq`), which consume the same event stream.

---

## Architecture

Skan is a single Go binary that is both a server-rendered web app and a
CQRS/event-sourcing participant on NATS JetStream.

```
  QR sticker  ──scan──▶  https://skan…/qr/{qrId}/{checksum}
                              │
                         ┌────▼──────────────────────┐  publish   ┌────────────────┐
                         │   Go binary (go/)         │ ─────────▶ │ NATS JetStream │
                         │   html/template pages     │            │   "NATHEJK"    │
                         │   commands/  (write)      │            └───────┬────────┘
                         │   table/     (read)       │◀── MySQL ◀─────────┘ replay
                         └───────────────────────────┘        (projections)
```

- **Write side** — `nathejk/commands/qr.go` publishes three events and never
  writes SQL directly: `qr.*.found`, `qr.*.registered`, `qr.*.scanned`.
- **Read side** — `nathejk/table/*` packages implement `streaminterface.Consumer`
  and materialise events into MariaDB. `xstream.Mux` wires them up; on every
  start the whole log is replayed, so the read model is disposable.
- Skan **consumes projections it does not own**: `patrulje`, `klan`, `senior`,
  `personnel` are populated from events published by other services (mainly
  `hq`/tilmelding). Skan owns only `qr` and `scan`.

### Photographs (`photo`, `photocover`)

`nathejk/table/photo` and `nathejk/table/photocover` are both wired up (task 005) and
drive the identity confirmation. They are also the **reference implementation of the
target shape** for projections in this org (see *The library migration* below), so
read them before writing a new one.

- `photo` is a **verbatim copy of the `foto` service's package**, bound for
  `shared-go`. Do not edit it; adapt around it. `photocover` is `hq`'s entity and
  must be constructed here with a **nil publisher** — choosing a cover is not a
  scanner's job.
- They store **content-hash refs, never bytes**, and `photo` never exposes the
  original (it carries the camera's metadata, including GPS). The bytes are served
  by the `foto` service at `<foto-base-url>/photos/<ref>`, e.g.
  `https://foto.local.nathejk.dk/photos/9706901b46b0…6bfb`. The base URL **must come
  from an environment variable** — never hardcode a host.
- **The photograph shown is the smallest rendition at least 1024px wide** (`photos.go`,
  `identificationRef`), falling back to the display `Ref` when no rendition qualifies.
  The 256px `thumbRef` was used at first to keep the page light on a field connection,
  and that was the wrong trade: the photograph *is* the identity check, and a scanner
  squinting at a list thumbnail cannot confidently say "no, that is not this patrol".
  On live data this costs 160 KB instead of 15 KB, and saves 380 KB against the 2000px
  display image.
- **`coverPhotoRef` deliberately stays the display ref**, not whichever rendition is
  rendered: it is the photograph's identity, carried on the registration form and
  compared in `doMapHandler`. Tying the confirmation to a rendition would break it the
  moment the rendition set changed.
- **A patrol cannot start the race without a photograph.** The race begins with the
  scouts receiving their first map, which does not happen until they have been
  photographed — so a photograph always exists by the time a QR code is registered.
  "No photograph" is therefore an error state, not a case to design a fallback for:
  refuse the registration and send the scanner to HQ (task 006).
- A **chosen cover is the exception**. Nothing may be publishing
  `photocoverselected` yet, so the fallback — the team's newest photograph — is the
  path that actually runs. `photographed` is confirmed present on the stream;
  `purged` may not be.

### The library migration

This repo's streaming and projection plumbing is **being replaced**, and the two
photo packages are already written against the new libraries:

| Legacy, in-repo | Replacement |
|---|---|
| `superfluids/` (`streaminterface`, `jetstream`, `xstream`) | `github.com/jrgensen/stream` |
| `pkg/tablerow`, `pkg/sqlpersister`, and more | `github.com/jrgensen/cqrs` |

All projections are destined for `github.com/nathejk/shared-go` once they have
stabilised. That is why `photo` must stay byte-identical to its origin.

**The switch is complete (task 007).** `superfluids/`, `pkg/tablerow` and
`pkg/sqlpersister` have been **deleted**, not left running alongside the replacements;
both `jrgensen/stream` and `jrgensen/cqrs` are in `go.mod` and every projection uses
them. What remains of "legacy" is a *file layout* difference, not a dependency — see
*Coding conventions*.

### Maps handed out (`kort`)

`nathejk/table/kort` is **hq's package**, copied in like `photo`: it owns the map sheets
printed for the race and the sets (`kortsaet`) they belong to. Do not edit it. It is
constructed with a **nil publisher** — sheets are drawn up in hq, and a scanner may only
choose among them.

- **The "spejder set" is the `kortsaet` marked for the `patrulje` team type.** There is no
  `spejder` team type; the word names the scouts, the type names the team they form. An
  unmarked set is the **crew** set and is deliberately *not* a fallback when choosing a
  patrol's sheet — a crew sheet would show the scouts checkpoints they should not see yet.
  Several sets may carry the same marking, so it is a filter, not a key.
- **`kort.Maps()` cannot be used here.** It resolves checkpoint lists and handout posts
  against the `checkpoint` and `checkgroup` projections; skan now has `checkpoint` but still
  no `checkgroup`, so it fails with `Table 'skan.checkgroup' doesn't exist`.
  `data.KortReader` asks the narrower questions skan has instead. Do not "fix" this by
  creating the table empty: `Maps()` would then succeed while reporting every sheet as having
  no checkpoints.
- **A scanner manning a handout post gets that post's sheet preselected** (task 019), via
  `checkpersonnel → checkpoint.checkgroupId → kort.handoutCheckgroupId`. Deliberately
  conservative: the shift's time range is honoured when set, the read is year-scoped, and
  **two candidate sheets mean no suggestion at all** rather than a guess. The suggestion is
  preselected and labelled, never applied silently.
- **The `checkpoint` and `checkpersonnel` projections are locally fixed and therefore
  diverge from hq's copies.** Both used a plain `INSERT` for their create/add events, so
  every replay re-inserted rows that already existed and dead-lettered them — 16 per boot
  here. The data stayed correct, which is what made it easy to miss, but it broke the
  `cqrs.Consumer` idempotency contract and put a permanent floor under a signal that is only
  useful at zero. Both are now upserts (`ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE` via goqu), which also
  replaced their `%q` quoting.
  **Re-copying either package from hq would silently revert this** — diff before you do, and
  push the fix upstream instead.
- Registering a code **requires** a sheet from the spejder set, validated server-side, and
  the chosen id travels on the `registered` event as `mapId` (`event.QrRegistered`).
- **A code can be re-bound within a year.** A discontinued patrol's remaining scouts are
  reassigned and bring their map, so `qr` folds with `ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE` (task 018)
  and the last registration wins. The guard against a careless re-point is the photo
  confirmation, plus the fact that the re-bind screen is only reachable when the current
  team is discontinued.
- **An unused code and a discontinued team's code are different situations and must never
  share wording.** `reassign` tells them apart: one has never been bound to a patrulje
  ("ikke tilknyttet en patrulje endnu", asks for a *patruljenummer*, "tilknyt kortet"), the
  other was bound to a team that has left the race ("udgået af løbet", asks for the
  *holdnummer* of whoever holds it now, "flyt kortet"). Every link and form on that page must
  carry `reassign` onward — dropping it silently turns a hand-over into a first registration
  and loses the sheet the scouts already carry.
- **A discontinued patrulje cannot be given a map either** (task 021). There are no active
  members to hand one to, so naming one is a mistyped number — most often the team the scouts
  *left* instead of the one they joined. `mapHandler` shows "Patruljen er udgået" and
  withdraws the confirmation; `doMapHandler` refuses with `424` independently, because the
  page may have been open while the last member was moved off. Note `map.html` now carries
  **two** "udgået" sentences about **two different teams** — the code's previous holder and
  the one just typed. Keep them distinct.
- **"Discontinued" is a started team with no active members left**:
  `signupStatus == STARTED && activeMemberCount == 0`, and `Patrulje.Discontinued()` is
  where that rule lives. Both halves matter — a team that has not started has no active
  members either. There is deliberately no event for it, and no reverse event: move a member
  back in and the count makes the team active again.
- **`patrulje.activeMemberCount` is written by the `spejderstatus` projection**, not by
  `patrulje`. Deliberate, and documented there: the mux gives no ordering guarantee between
  consumers, so recomputing the count in `patrulje` could count member rows that had not
  been written yet. Do not "tidy" it into the owning consumer.
- Two earlier encodings of discontinuation are **wrong**: there are no
  `patrulje.*.status.changed` events on the stream at all (though `klan.*.status.changed`
  exists, which makes the absence easy to misread), and `NathejkTeamMerged` /
  `patruljemerged` is deprecated — it stored the conclusion rather than the input, which is
  why it needed `.merged` *and* `.splited`.

### Event bodies this service extends (`nathejk/event`)

Three fields live here because `shared-go` has nowhere for them, all added **additively**
so consumers decoding into the shared bodies are unaffected:

| Field | On | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `locationSource` | `qr.*.scanned` | GPS vs a hand-placed marker |
| `locationAccuracy` | `qr.*.scanned` | metres of confidence; empty for a manual marker |
| `mapId` | `qr.*.registered` | which sheet was handed over |

All three should be **upstreamed into `shared-go`** so hq and the map tooling can rely on
them. `event.SeniorUpdated` is a different case: it *restores* a `teamId` that shared-go
dropped while publishers kept sending it — that one is a bug to fix upstream, not a field
to add. Note **only the first `senior.updated` carries `teamId`** (that event is really
"senior added", and a senior may not change klan), so a consumer must treat an empty value
as "not stated" and keep what it knows — never as "no klan".

### Finding out what the stream actually contains

Before adding a subscription on a guess, list the subjects:

```sh
docker run --rm --network jetstream natsio/nats-box:latest \
  nats -s nats://jetstream:4222 stream subjects NATHEJK
```

This repo has twice wasted a replay cycle guessing at subjects that do not exist — there
are no `patrulje.*.status.changed` and no merge events, though `klan.*.status.changed`
exists, which makes the absence easy to misread.

### Event subjects

Pattern: `NATHEJK.{year}.{entity}.{id}.{event}`

The year comes from the **required `YEAR` environment variable** (dev: `2026`),
threaded through `commands.New`. There is deliberately no default — a wrong year
fails silently, because year-keyed reads simply find nothing. Hardcoded years are
not allowed.

Published by this repo:

| Subject | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `NATHEJK.{year}.qr.{qrId}.found` | An unregistered code was scanned for the first time |
| `NATHEJK.{year}.qr.{qrId}.registered` | A code was bound to a patrol |
| `NATHEJK.{year}.qr.{qrId}.scanned` | A registered code was scanned (with location) |

Note `SubjectFromStr` replaces the **first** `:` with `.`, so `NATHEJK:2025.qr…`
and `NATHEJK.2025.qr…` are the same subject. Both spellings appear in the code;
prefer the `.` form in new code.

### Request flow

**There is no manual scanning.** This service records scans of real QR codes only. A
team-number entry form (`POST /`) used to exist and was **deliberately removed**
(task 011) — do not reintroduce a way to record a scan without a code. The only writer
of scans is `PUT /register`, which always has a real QR id.

| Route | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `GET /` | Landing page — says how scanning works, and is where a scanner logs in |
| `GET /qr/{id}/{cs}` | **Scan landing page.** Unknown code → publishes `found`, redirects to `/map/…`. Code whose patrulje is discontinued → redirects to `/map/…?reassign=1`. Otherwise renders the confirmation/geolocation page |
| `POST /qr/{id}/{cs}` | Login form target for the scan page |
| `GET /map/{id}/{cs}` | Registration page — asks for / confirms the team number |
| `POST /map/{id}/{cs}` | Publishes `registered`, redirects back to `/qr/…` |
| `PUT /register` | JSON endpoint the scan page calls with geolocation → publishes `scanned` |
| `POST /login`, `GET /logout` | Phone-number login (see below) |
| `GET /qr?n=N` | Generates a CSV of `id,url` for the first N codes — the sticker-printing feed. Token-protected, **not** login-protected |
| `GET /geo` | JSON scan dump with coordinates, for GIS/map export. Token-protected, **not** login-protected |
| `GET /about`, `GET /healthcheck` | Static page / health probe |
| `GET /*` | Static files from `/webroot` |

### QR identity and checksum

QR ids are plain incrementing integers. Each URL carries an adler32 checksum of
`"{qrId}:{SECRET}"` (`main.Checksum`) so codes cannot be enumerated by hand.
Handlers reject a mismatched checksum with `417 Expectation Failed`. **`SECRET`
is a required environment variable** — changing it invalidates every printed
sticker.

### Authentication and roles

There is no external auth service here (unlike `hq`). `internal/login`:

- `POST /login` takes a phone number, looks it up in the `personnel` projection
  and then the `senior` projection, and stores the resulting user as a
  **base64-encoded JSON cookie** named `user`.
- `Authenticate(next, login)` renders the login page instead of the target when
  the cookie is absent.
- The cookie is **not signed, not HttpOnly and not Secure** — it is trivially
  forgeable. Treat this as a known, deliberate-for-now trade-off; do not build
  anything security-sensitive on top of it without raising it first.

**A scanner has exactly one role, and it is derived from the phone number.** The
older PHP app assigned people roles they could switch between per session
(`twig/choose.twig`); that concept is **gone and is not coming back**. Do not
reintroduce role selection, a role switcher, or a role stored in the session.

The rules, **implemented in `internal/login` (task 001)**:

| Phone found in | Role |
|---|---|
| `personnel` only | crew |
| `senior` only | bandit |
| **both** | **refuse the login** |
| neither | refuse the login |

**"Senior" and "bandit" are the same thing** — a senior signed up to a klan *is* a
bandit, so presence in the `senior` projection is the whole bandit test. There is
no per-person flag to consult and no subset of seniors that does something else.
(`geoHandler` already relies on this, labelling every senior `"Bandit"`.)

A phone number registered as both crew and senior is a **data error upstream**,
not a case to disambiguate in the UI. Do not guess, and do not prefer one table
over the other: block the login and tell the user (in Danish) to contact HQ so
one of the two registrations can be removed. Note that today's `userByPhone`
does exactly the wrong thing here — it returns on the first `personnel` hit and
never notices the conflict.

