## Project structure

```
skan/
├── docker-compose.yml       # dev stack: api, mysql, adminer, redis, gw
├── docker/Dockerfile        # multistage: base → dev (air) / build → prod
├── webroot/                 # static assets served at /
└── go/                      # module nathejk.dk, Go 1.24 — the whole app
    ├── main.go              # wiring: jetstream, db, projections, mux, commands
    ├── app.go               # config (flags + env), http.Server
    ├── routes.go            # chi router + ALL handlers and templates parsing
    ├── database.go, env.go, server.go
    ├── templates/           # html/template pages (embedded via //go:embed)
    ├── internal/
    │   ├── login/           # phone-number login + cookie auth
    │   ├── data/            # Models struct: the read-side interfaces
    │   ├── logging/, jsonlog/, validator/, vcs/
    ├── nathejk/
    │   ├── commands/        # write side (qr.go is the live one; team.go is inherited)
    │   └── table/           # projections, all on jrgensen/cqrs:
    │                     #   owned:  qr, scan
    │                     #   copied: patrulje, klan, senior, personnel, photo,
    │                     #           photocover, kort, spejderstatus,
    │                     #           checkpoint, checkpersonnel
    │                     #   tables.Quote / tables.Datetime live in table/sql.go
    └── maps.go, photos.go   # handler-side helpers for kort and photo
```

Several files were **inherited from sibling Nathejk repos and unused here**; they
have been **deleted** (task 012) — `nathejk/table/{spejder,signup,payment,scanner}`,
the loose `pincode`/`registrant`/`spejderstatus`/`patruljestatus`/`confirm` files,
`nathejk/commands/{team,types}.go`, and
`internal/data/{teams,members,users,tokens,permissions,signup,filters}.go`.
`nathejk/table/errors.go` was kept, because `tables.ErrRecordNotFound` is live.
`data.Models` is now exactly the six projections this service reads.

### Legacy note

This app was a PHP/Twig application before it was ported to Go. A `go/twig/`
directory held the pre-port originals as an untracked local reference; it has been
**deleted**. Everything worth keeping from it is captured in *What each role may
see* below. Do not recreate it.

---

## What each role may see

**Rule of thumb: bandits may only learn about bandits. It has to be a fair game.**

A bandit is a player. Anything a bandit sees about a patrol's progress through the
race is an unfair advantage, so a bandit's view is restricted to information their
own side produced: their own catches, and other bandits' catches of that patrol.
Never show a bandit checkpoint activity, total scan counts, positions from crew
scans, or anything derived from them.

Crew are not players. A crew member may be checkpoint personnel, a guide helping
the scouts, or a samarit patching up blisters — all of them legitimately need the
full picture. **Crew may see everything.**

| | Bandit | Crew |
|---|---|---|
| The patrol's identity, name, photo | yes | yes |
| **Expected head count** — current strength | yes | yes |
| `catchCount` — times caught by bandits | yes | yes |
| Total scan count (all scanners) | **no** | yes |
| Checkpoint / crew scan activity, positions | **no** | yes |
| Free-text remark about the patrol | yes | yes |

Definitions:

- **expected head count** — `patrulje.activeMemberCount`, the patrol's **current** strength.
  Shown to both roles and shown prominently, because the scanner has to check it against the
  scouts in front of them. It is not race progress.
- **`catchCount`** — how many times the patrol has been **caught by bandits**,
  i.e. the number of scans of that patrol whose scanner is a senior. This is the
  one count a bandit is allowed to know.
- **scan count** — total scans by anyone, crew included. Crew-only.

### Strength changes in both directions

A patrol that falls below **three** members may not continue alone — the rest are reassigned
to other teams — so a team can be **larger** than it started as well as smaller. Two
consequences:

- "Expected" must be `activeMemberCount`, never `memberCount` (the start roster).
- The **armband encodes the start count** (`armNumber` is `teamNumber-memberCount`), so once
  strength changes the armband is permanently out of date. That is why a difference gets a
  yellow *vær opmærksom* note naming the direction (task 020): without it a scanner counting
  heads against the armband draws the wrong conclusion either way.

When adding anything to the scan-result page, decide which column of that table it
belongs in *before* writing the handler. Default to crew-only if unsure.

### Counting catches: the rescan rule

A rescan **counts**. Bandits legitimately catch the same patrol more than once,
so `catchCount` is a plain count of scans, not of distinct scanners.

But accidental double scans must not inflate it. The guard — **decided, not yet
implemented**:

> Look at the patrol's **single most recent scan**. If it was made by the *same*
> scanner *less than 30 minutes* ago, ask for an explicit confirmation
> ("skal dette tælle som en ny scanning?") before recording. Otherwise record it
> straight away.

Consequences of that phrasing, all intended:

| Situation | Result |
|---|---|
| Same scanner, same patrol, 5 min apart, nothing in between | confirmation required |
| Same scanner, same patrol, 45 min apart | recorded, no question |
| Same scanner twice, but another scanner scanned in between | recorded, no question |
| A different scanner scans right after | recorded, no question |

Rules for implementing it:

- The check is **server-side**, on the patrol's scan history — not on the browser,
  and not on the QR code (a patrol may carry a new map with a new code).
- A confirmation must never *drop* the scan silently. Unconfirmed means "not
  recorded yet, ask the user", and the user must be able to say yes and have it
  count.
- The existing flow is `GET /qr/{id}/{cs}` → page → `PUT /register` with the
  position. So the natural shape is for `PUT /register` to answer "this needs
  confirming" and for the page to re-`PUT` with an explicit confirm flag. Do not
  move the decision into the template.
- This needs a new read on the `scan` projection (latest scan for a team); today's
  querier only has `GetAll` and a broken `GetByID`.

### Protecting `/qr` and `/geo`

These two are machine endpoints, not scanner pages: `/qr` feeds the sticker
printing and `/geo` feeds map/GIS export. They are **not** behind the phone login
— they are protected by a **secret token passed as a GET parameter**, checked
before anything else runs.

- Use a **separate environment variable** for it, *not* `SECRET`. `SECRET` seeds
  the QR checksum; a token travels in URLs, browser history, proxy logs and
  `Referer` headers, and leaking `SECRET` would let anyone mint valid sticker URLs
  for every code. Keep the two unrelated.
- Compare with `crypto/subtle.ConstantTimeCompare`, and refuse when the token is
  empty or the env var is unset — never fail open.
- Respond `404` to a bad or missing token, so the endpoint's existence isn't
  advertised.
- These endpoints stay crew-grade information. Do not hand the token to bandits;
  `/geo` is a live map of the whole race and would break the fair-game rule
  outright.

**Both are now protected** by `EXPORT_TOKEN` (task 002): a missing, empty or wrong
token gets `404`, and an unset env var refuses everything. Do not add new endpoints
without deciding which guard they belong behind.

### Role-dependent reads are the handler's job

`scanResultData` in `routes.go` is the single place the bandit/crew split is applied,
and `internal/login.User.IsBandit()` is the only source of the role. Two rules:

- Omit crew-only values from the template data entirely for a bandit. Do not pass them
  and hide them in markup — the pre-port page did that with a `display:hidden` div, and
  data on a player's phone is data they have.
- Do not even *query* a crew-only figure when the scanner is a bandit.

---

## Technology stack

- **Go 1.24**, module `nathejk.dk`, single binary, no separate `cmd/` dir
- **Router**: `go-chi/chi/v5`
- **Views**: `html/template`, parsed per request from an `embed.FS`, each page
  composed as `base.html` + one page template
- **Client-side**: no build step. Bootstrap 3 + jQuery from CDN, plus inline
  `<script>` for geolocation and the `PUT /register` fetch
- **DB**: MariaDB 10.8, `go-sql-driver/mysql`, hand-written SQL
- **Events**: NATS JetStream via `github.com/jrgensen/stream` (`jetstream`, `xstream`),
  with projections on `github.com/jrgensen/cqrs`. The in-repo `superfluids/` and
  `pkg/tablerow` are gone (task 007)
- **Shared domain types/messages**: `github.com/nathejk/shared-go` — also where
  projections go once they stabilise
- **Dev loop**: `air` (`go/go.air.toml`) as a Go tool — rebuilds and reruns tests
  on change
- **Quality gates in the prod build**: `go test`, `staticcheck`, `govulncheck`
  all run in the `build` stage, so a lint or vuln failure fails the image

`redis` / `REDIS_ADDR` are provisioned in compose but not used by the Go code.

---

## Development

```sh
docker compose up          # api + mysql + adminer + redis + redis-commander
```

Requires the external `traefik` and `jetstream` networks from the org infra repo.
Everything runs in containers — never run `go` on the host. One-offs:
`docker compose run --rm api go test ./...`.

| Service | URL |
|---|---|
| App | `https://skan.local.nathejk.dk` |
| Adminer | `http://mysql.skan.local.nathejk.dk` |
| Redis Commander | `http://redis.skan.local.nathejk.dk` |

### Environment

| Variable | Dev value | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `YEAR` | `2026` | event year slug. **Required, no default** — an unset value stops the process at boot. Overridable with `-year` |
| `EXPORT_TOKEN` | `dev-export-token` | **Required.** Guards `/qr` and `/geo` as a `?token=` parameter. Never reuse `SECRET` here |
| `FOTO_BASE_URL` | `https://foto.local.nathejk.dk` | **Required.** Photograph bytes live at `<base>/photos/<ref>` |
| `JETSTREAM_DSN` | `nats://jetstream:4222` | the local broker on the external `jetstream` network |
| `DB_DSN` | `root:ib@tcp(mysql:3306)/skan?parseTime=true&multiStatements=true` | `multiStatements` is required by `spejderstatus`, whose `table.sql` declares two tables in one statement. It also enables stacked queries, so consumer quoting matters more — see `tables.Quote` |
| `SECRET` | `nh2025` | seeds the QR checksum; required. **Never** put this in a URL |
| `WEBROOT` | `/www` (flag default) | compose mounts `/webroot`; `routes.go` hardcodes `/webroot` |
| `SMS_DSN`, `REDIS_ADDR` | set | currently unused by the code |

Tables are created on boot with `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`, so **adding a
column to a `table.sql` does nothing where the table already exists** — drop the
table (or the DB) in dev. Note also that a replay **upserts rather than truncating**: a
hand-edited row survives a restart and only disappears once the table is dropped.

### Verifying a change against the running service

Assumptions about this app have been wrong often enough that manual verification against
real data is expected, not optional. What works:

```sh
# Run the whole gate the way the prod image does.
docker compose run --rm --no-deps -T --entrypoint sh api \
  -c 'go build ./... && go test ./... && go tool staticcheck ./... && go tool govulncheck ./...'

# Query the read model.
docker compose exec -T mysql mysql -uroot -pib skan -e 'select * from qr;'

# Fetch a page or PUT JSON. Use curl, not the api container's BusyBox wget:
# wget has no cookie jar and no --max-redirect, and fails by printing nothing,
# which reads exactly like a passing test.
docker run --rm --network skan_local curlimages/curl:latest -s \
  -H 'Cookie: user=…' 'http://api/qr/3/163774989'
```

- **Restarting `api` takes 75–95 s** — `air` runs the tests before rebuilding. Wait for
  `Server started` in `docker compose logs api` before testing.
- Sticker URLs, including the checksum, come from `GET /qr?n=N&token=dev-export-token`.
- The login cookie is unsigned base64 JSON, so a role can be forged for testing:
  `{"ID":"c1","Phone":"11111111","Type":"gøgler","Role":"crew"}` for crew,
  `{"ID":"b1","Phone":"22222222","Type":"klan","Role":"bandit"}` for a bandit.
- Assertions on rendered HTML must strip tags and normalise whitespace — see
  `visibleText`/`squashSpace` in `routes_test.go`. `Der skal være <strong>7</strong>
  spejdere` is one sentence to a reader and three fragments to `strings.Contains`.

---

## Known deviations & rough edges

Flag or fix these deliberately; don't quietly copy the pattern.

- **Compose no longer uses a gateway container.** Each web-exposed service declares
  its own Traefik labels and joins the external `traefik` network (task 009).
  `api` **serves both HTTP and HTTPS** (org pattern 3) with the `desec` cert resolver
  on the `websecure` router only. It deliberately does **not** redirect HTTP→HTTPS:
  `redirectscheme` answers 302, and a client following a 302 on a POST turns it into a
  GET and drops the body — which would silently lose a scan, since every scan is a
  `POST /`, `POST /map/{id}/{cs}` or `PUT /register`. The redirect is unnecessary here
  anyway, because `qrHandler` prints `https://` sticker URLs, so scanners arrive over
  TLS and get their secure context for `navigator.geolocation` without one.
- **A router with any `tls` setting is HTTPS-only.** Keep the cert resolver on the
  `-secure` router alone; putting it on a single shared router makes plain HTTP match
  nothing and collect Traefik's own `text/plain` 404, which looks like the app is down.
- **Both routers must name their service explicitly**, and the service must exist.
  Traefik's implicit service for this container is `api-skan`, so
  `traefik.http.services.skan.loadbalancer.server.port: "80"` is present purely to
  *name* the service `skan` — without it both routers are disabled with
  `the service "skan@docker" does not exist`.
- **There is no shared `redirect-to-https@docker` middleware** on the running Traefik,
  despite what the org-wide rules say. Sibling repos declare their own
  (`hq-redirect-to-https@docker`). Referencing the shared name disables the router —
  `tilmelding@docker` is currently broken this way.
- **The `prod` image is not a compose service.** Building it runs the full
  test/lint/vuln gate with no source mount, so `docker compose up` used to build and
  fail on it. Build it explicitly:
  `docker build -f docker/Dockerfile --target prod -t skan:local .`
- **`JETSTREAM_DSN` now points at the local broker** (`nats://jetstream:4222`) rather
  than the shared dev stream (task 009).
- **Rescans are guarded** (task 004): the same scanner scanning the same patrulje
  within 30 minutes gets a confirmation prompt via a `409` from `PUT /register`. The
  `scan` primary key is `(qrId, uts, scannerId)`, so only the *same* scanner twice in
  one second is deduped — a double-submit, not two catches.
- The geolocation fallback shows a Leaflet map and records the marker with
  `locationSource=manual` (task 016). A GPS fix also records `locationAccuracy` in
  metres; a hand-placed marker deliberately has none, because there is no measurement
  behind it. Scans from before these fields exist carry `""`, which must **not** be read
  as GPS. Both live in `nathejk/event` as additive properties on the shared body — they
  should be upstreamed into `shared-go` so hq and the map tooling can rely on them.
- **`navigator.geolocation` may never call back at all**, so the page carries its own
  15 s watchdog (task 022). A `timeout` option is not enough: the spec stops that clock
  while the permission prompt is unanswered, so a scanner who taps neither allow nor deny
  hangs on "Finder position" forever with an **empty console** — nothing failed, as far as
  the browser is concerned. A single `claimed` flag arbitrates between the fix, the
  watchdog and the scanner's own "skip to map" link, so one scan cannot be recorded twice.
  Any new path that finishes a scan must respect it.
- **Never leave the spinner up on a failure.** A `PUT` that rejects must say the scan was
  not recorded: a spinner reads as "still working", and a scanner walks away from a lost
  scan believing it landed.
- **A client-supplied `source` is a claim, not a fact.** `event.NormalizeSource` maps it
  onto the known set and anything else to `""`, at the edge *and* again in the consumer,
  so a replay does not trust what an older publisher put on the stream. Accuracy is
  parsed to whole metres for the same reason.
- A copied projection may bring assumptions with it. `spejderstatus` needs
  `multiStatements=true` in the DSN (its `table.sql` declares two tables and is consumed as
  one statement, where `kort` embeds its second schema separately), and its test file trips
  `U1000`, suppressed by a per-package `staticcheck.conf` rather than by editing a file that
  must stay identical to its origin.
- Consumers quote SQL values with `tables.Quote`/`tables.Datetime` (task 013). **Never
  use `%q`** — it emits a Go string literal, not a SQL one, and on a `time.Time` it
  invokes the Stringer and writes ` +0000 UTC` into your column.
- A handler that publishes an event and then redirects to a page which reads the
  resulting projection must wait for it to catch up — see `waitForRegistration`
  (task 015). `stream/caughtup` is a boot sentinel and cannot help with a single write.
- There is **no per-patrol remark**. `coordinates.html` used to reference `.remark`
  (the PHP app's `noticeText`, shown in red to both roles) but no projection holds such
  a field, so the markup was removed rather than faked. Reintroducing it needs somewhere
  for the text to live first.
- The year slug used by the projections' `year` **column** is a separate concern
  from `YEAR`: a projection must take it from the message subject, so a replay of an
  older year is not relabelled with the current one. `qr` and `scan` now do this;
  check any new projection does too.
- **`go build`, `go test`, `staticcheck` and `govulncheck` all pass.** Keep it that way:
  `air` runs the tests before each rebuild, so a broken build stops the dev loop, and the
  production image's `build` stage runs all four.
- A bad *message* is dead-lettered rather than fatal, but a projection that cannot create
  its table at boot still calls `log.Fatalf` — deliberately, since a missing table would
  otherwise fail on every message.
- `deadletter.Count()` counts **every** unresolved row ever recorded, not just the current
  replay, so `TRUNCATE deadletter` before using it to judge a change.

